.bal{display:grid;gap:0}.bal small{font:13px var(--mono);color:var(--muted)}.bal b{font:700 38px var(--mono)}
.bad-t{color:var(--bad)}
.tablewrap{overflow-x:auto;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;background:var(--panel)}
.cmp{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;min-width:560px;font-size:15px}
.cmp th,.cmp td{text-align:left;padding:10px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);vertical-align:top}
.cmp th{font:700 12px var(--body);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted)}
.cmp tr:last-child td{border-bottom:0}
.three{display:grid;gap:14px;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr))}
.three>div{display:grid;gap:8px;align-content:start;min-width:0}
.three p{margin:0;color:var(--muted);font-size:15px}
footer{padding-block:40px 0;border-top:1px solid var(--line);color:var(--muted);max-width:44rem}
@keyframes pop{from{transform:scale(.85);opacity:.3}}
@keyframes lit{from{background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink);border-color:var(--line);transform:none;opacity:.6}}
@keyframes drop{from{transform:translateY(-14px);opacity:0}}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){*{animation-duration:.01ms!important;animation-delay:0s!important;transition-duration:.01ms!important}}
</style>

<div class="wrap">
<nav class="side" id="nav" aria-label="Lessons"></nav>
<main>
<div class="hero">
  <h1>Python, drawn <span class="py">step by step</span></h1>
  <p><span id="count">16</span> short lessons from your first variable to classes and objects. Press Play to watch code run, or change the inputs and try it yourself.</p>
</div>

<div class="part" data-part="Basics">
<section class="lesson" id="variables">
  <span class="tag">Basics</span><h2>Variables and types</h2>
  <p class="lead">A variable is a <b>name that points at a value</b>. Python works out the type from the value, and you can point the same name at something new at any time.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="vars"></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson" id="operators">
  <span class="tag">Basics</span><h2>Operators</h2>
  <p class="lead">Change <b>a</b> and <b>b</b> and watch every operator answer at once. Notice that <code>/</code> always gives a float, while <code>//</code> rounds down.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="ops"></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson" id="strings">
  <span class="tag">Basics</span><h2>Strings and slicing</h2>
  <p class="lead">A string is a row of characters, each with an index counted from the left (<code>0, 1, 2…</code>) and from the right (<code>-1, -2…</code>). A slice <code>s[start:stop:step]</code> includes <b>start</b> and stops <b>before stop</b>.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="slice"></div>
</section>
</div>

<div class="part" data-part="Collections">
<section class="lesson" id="lists">
  <span class="tag">Collections</span><h2>Lists</h2>
  <p class="lead">A list is an ordered, changeable row of values. Most list methods change the list itself instead of returning a new one.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="lists"></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson" id="dicts">
  <span class="tag">Collections</span><h2>Dictionaries, tuples and sets</h2>
  <p class="lead">A dictionary looks up a value by its <b>key</b>, which is much faster than searching a list. Pick the collection by how you will use it.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="dicts"></div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="cmp">
    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Written</th><th>Ordered</th><th>Changeable</th><th>Duplicates</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>list</code></td><td><code>[1, 2, 2]</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Allowed</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>tuple</code></td><td><code>(1, 2, 2)</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Allowed</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>set</code></td><td><code>{1, 2}</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Removed</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>dict</code></td><td><code>{"a": 1}</code></td><td>Keeps insertion order</td><td>Yes</td><td>Keys are unique</td></tr>
  </table></div>
</section>
</div>

<div class="part" data-part="Control flow">
<section class="lesson" id="conditionals">
  <span class="tag">Control flow</span><h2>if, elif and else</h2>
  <p class="lead">Python checks each condition from the top and runs the <b>first one that is True</b>. Drag the slider to move the score across the thresholds.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="cond"></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson" id="loops">
  <span class="tag">Control flow</span><h2>for and while loops</h2>
  <p class="lead">A <code>for</code> loop walks through a sequence. A <code>while</code> loop repeats as long as its condition stays True, so something inside must eventually make it False.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="for"></div>
  <div class="mount" data-w="while"></div>
</section>
</div>

<div class="part" data-part="Functions">
<section class="lesson" id="functions">
  <span class="tag">Functions</span><h2>Functions and the call stack</h2>
  <p class="lead">Each call gets its own frame with its own local names. Frames pile up while functions call other functions, then disappear as each one returns.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="func"></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson" id="comprehensions">
  <span class="tag">Functions</span><h2>List comprehensions</h2>
  <p class="lead">A comprehension builds a new list in one line: take each item, optionally filter it with <code>if</code>, and transform what is left.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="comp"></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson" id="exceptions">
  <span class="tag">Functions</span><h2>Handling errors</h2>
  <p class="lead">When something fails, Python jumps out of the <code>try</code> block to the matching <code>except</code>. <code>finally</code> always runs. Type a value and see which lines run.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="exc"></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson" id="modules">
  <span class="tag">Functions</span><h2>Modules and files</h2>
  <p class="lead">A module is a Python file you can reuse. The <code>with</code> statement opens a file and closes it for you, even if an error happens.</p>
  <div class="three">
    <div><pre class="code" data-static>import math
print(math.sqrt(16))</pre><p>Import a whole module and use its names with a dot. Prints <code>4.0</code>.</p></div>
    <div><pre class="code" data-static>from random import choice
print(choice(["a", "b"]))</pre><p>Import just the name you need. Prints one of the two items at random.</p></div>
    <div><pre class="code" data-static>with open("notes.txt") as f:
    for line in f:
        print(line.strip())</pre><p>Read a text file line by line. The file closes when the block ends.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<div class="part" data-part="Object-oriented">
<section class="lesson" id="classes">
  <span class="tag">Object-oriented</span><h2>Classes and objects</h2>
  <p class="lead">A class is a blueprint. Calling it builds an <b>object</b> with its own data. <code>self</code> is the object a method is working on.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="classes"></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson" id="inheritance">
  <span class="tag">Object-oriented</span><h2>Inheritance</h2>
  <p class="lead">A subclass starts with everything its parent has. When you call a method, Python looks in the object's class first, then up the chain. Pick a class and a method to see the search.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="inherit"></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson" id="polymorphism">
  <span class="tag">Object-oriented</span><h2>Polymorphism</h2>
  <p class="lead">Different classes can answer the same method call in their own way. The loop does not care what each object is, only that it has <code>speak()</code>.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="poly"></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson" id="encapsulation">
  <span class="tag">Object-oriented</span><h2>Encapsulation</h2>
  <p class="lead">Keep an object's data behind methods so it cannot be put in a bad state. A leading underscore marks <code>_balance</code> as internal, and a property exposes it read-only.</p>
  <div class="mount" data-w="bank"></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson" id="dunder">
  <span class="tag">Object-oriented</span><h2>Special methods</h2>
  <p class="lead">Methods with double underscores let your objects work with Python's own syntax: printing, <code>len()</code>, <code>==</code> and <code>+</code>.</p>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="cmp">
    <tr><th>Method</th><th>Runs when you write</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>__init__</code></td><td><code>Dog("Rex")</code></td><td>Setting up a new object</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>__str__</code></td><td><code>print(obj)</code></td><td>Readable text</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>__repr__</code></td><td><code>repr(obj)</code>, the debugger</td><td>Exact, unambiguous text</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>__len__</code></td><td><code>len(obj)</code></td><td>Size of a container</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>__eq__</code></td><td><code>a == b</code></td><td>Custom equality</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>__add__</code></td><td><code>a + b</code></td><td>Custom addition</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <div class="three">
    <div><pre class="code" data-static>class Vec:
    def __init__(self, x, y):
        self.x, self.y = x, y

    def __add__(self, other):
        return Vec(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)

    def __repr__(self):
        return f"Vec({self.x}, {self.y})"

print(Vec(1, 2) + Vec(3, 4))</pre><p>Output: <code>Vec(4, 6)</code>. The <code>+</code> calls <code>__add__</code>, and print uses <code>__repr__</code> because there is no <code>__str__</code>.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
</div>

<footer>
  <p>Next topics worth learning: generators, decorators, dataclasses, type hints and the standard library modules <code>json</code>, <code>pathlib</code> and <code>collections</code>.</p>
</footer>
</main>
</div>

<script>
const $=(s,r=document)=>r.querySelector(s);
const E=s=>String(s).replace(/&/g,'&amp;').replace(/</g,'&lt;').replace(/>/g,'&gt;');
const KW=/^(def|class|return|if|elif|else|for|while|in|not|and|or|import|from|as|try|except|finally|with|raise|pass|break|continue|None|True|False|self|lambda|is)$/;
function hl(line){
  const re=/(#.*$)|("[^"]*"|'[^']*')|(\b\d+(?:\.\d+)?\b)|([A-Za-z_]\w*)|([\s\S])/g;
  let out='',m;
  while((m=re.exec(line))){
    if(m[1])out+='<i class="tc">'+E(m[1])+'</i>';
    else if(m[2])out+='<i class="ts">'+E(m[2])+'</i>';
    else if(m[3])out+='<i class="tn">'+m[3]+'</i>';
    else if(m[4])out+=KW.test(m[4])?'<i class="tk">'+m[4]+'</i>':E(m[4]);
